pancake is one library you can hold three ways. It is a [Zig](https://ziglang.org/)
package you import directly, a plain C ABI you can call from any FFI host, and four
language bindings that wrap that C ABI in a native idiom. Same core underneath, so
you choose whichever matches the language you are writing in.

[One core under all three surfaces, so the one you hold never changes what a session puts on the wire.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-surfaces.svg'))

| Surface | You write | Reach for it when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Zig package]($link.page('start')) | Zig | you are in Zig, or want the full engine and its types |
| [C ABI](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/consuming-from-c.md) | C, or any FFI host | you are embedding in C, or wrapping from a runtime with no binding yet |
| [Bindings]($link.page('bindings')) | Rust, TypeScript, Python, Racket | you are in one of those four languages |

## The Zig package

`zig fetch --save git+https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake`, import `pancake`,
and you have the application surface: the owning `Session` and its small verb
vocabulary, plus everything beneath it, down to the raw engine. The
[Start building]($link.page('start')) page walks a first round-trip line by line,
and the [examples](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/README.md)
read top-down from that surface to the engine, one `step` at a time. The exact
declarations you import, with their ownership and lifetime notes, are the generated
[Zig API reference]($link.page('reference/api')).

## The C ABI

The same code compiles to a set of composable archives, linked à la carte, each in
static and shared form (`.a` plus the platform's shared library). The base
`libpancake_ocapn` carries the sans-io peer, and each transport is its own archive
and header beside it. Every header is generated from the same Zig declarations its
archive compiles, so a header can never silently disagree with the binary. The
surface is deliberately boring: opaque handles, fixed-width return codes,
`ptr + len` views, callbacks, and explicit release functions, so a consumer can
wrap it well without knowing any Zig. The full archive-and-header inventory lives
on the [API reference]($link.page('reference')) page. The consuming-from-C guide
linked in the table above walks the build, the ownership rules, and a first complete
program.

## The bindings

A binding already did the C wrapping and presents an idiomatic API. See
[Bindings]($link.page('bindings')) for the shared shape, per-language tours, and how
each one dogfoods the archives through its own `*-dogfood` step.

## One library, one build, one engine

There is no separate "C build" or "Zig build" of the protocol. A Zig peer, a C
consumer, and a peer written against any binding hold the same OCapN conversation,
byte for byte. A session does negotiate its codec and CapTP version, and it settles
that with whoever answers, not by which surface you built on. One instance can
therefore run many sessions speaking different codecs at once; the
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) page walks that handshake.

Every artifact above is produced by `zig build`. `zig build check` runs the
in-tree build check: tests, protocol reference fixtures, examples, and the
structure checks. The upstream OCapN suite (`zig build ocapn-conformance`),
cross-language binding checks, and live interop run as sibling steps, since
they need non-Zig runtimes on PATH. The build steps in the
[README](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/README.md#build)
cover the common focused check steps; the full inventory lives in
[CONTRIBUTING](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/CONTRIBUTING.md#verifying-changes).

The one place the surfaces diverge is encryption. iroh rides a separate optional
artifact, which the [Transports]($link.page('transports')) page covers.
